# Adulis Seller Console — response headers (Cloudflare Pages `_headers` format).
#
# ADU-CON-02 v3.0 section 3.9 makes this file load-bearing: the entire policy
# protecting the payout surface lives here, statically, so that it is
# REVIEWABLE IN A DIFF rather than assembled at runtime by middleware.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE IS NO NONCE, AND THE ONE DIVERGENCE TO KNOW ABOUT
#
# ADU-SEC-01 v1.0 section 5.2 specifies:
#     script-src 'self' 'nonce-{NONCE}' 'strict-dynamic'
# ADU-CON-02 v3.0 section 3.9 supersedes that for this origin:
#     script-src 'self'
#
# The reason is the Vite-over-Next.js ruling. Next.js injects inline bootstrap
# scripts, which forces the nonce machinery of ADU-SEC-01 section 8. A Vite SPA
# emits ZERO inline scripts, so 'self' alone is sufficient — and 'self' has no
# bypass on an origin that serves only CI-built assets, because there is no
# upload path and no dynamic route on which an attacker-controlled file could
# ever be served.
#
# STATUS: SPEC_VERSION_GAP. ADU-SEC-01 -> v1.1 is pending (ADU-CON-02 section 23)
# and must absorb this nonce-free console policy along with the second
# __Host-adulis_device cookie from R17. Until that reissue lands, this file
# follows ADU-CON-02 v3.0 section 3.9 because section 9 of
# .claude/reference/DOCUMENT_AUTHORITY.md makes v3.0 govern build sequencing —
# and the Tier 0 work order directs this form explicitly.
#
# DOCUMENTED REOPEN TRIGGER (ADU-CON-02 section 3.9), and it is the whole
# argument: the moment the console origin ever serves user-influenced content,
# 'self' stops being safe and the nonce policy of ADU-SEC-01 section 5.2 must be
# restored. Serving one user-supplied file from this origin reopens this ruling.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every other directive is ADU-SEC-01 v1.0 section 5.2 unchanged:
#   default-src 'none'               deny by default; every allowance is named
#   style-src   'unsafe-inline'      the section 9 concession, scoped to styles only
#   img-src     blob:                required by the payment evidence viewer
#                                    (section 7 / R18). Evidence is never at a
#                                    predictable URL, so blob: appears here and
#                                    NOWHERE else.
#   connect-src the API origin       the console holds no credential; it learns
#                                    everything by calling the API
#   worker-src  'self'               a poisoned service worker is persistent XSS
#                                    that survives reload, so its source is pinned
#   frame-src / frame-ancestors      the console frames nothing and is framed by
#               'none'               nothing. Social embeds belong on storefronts.
#   require-trusted-types-for        kills DOM XSS sinks (innerHTML, eval) in
#               'script'             supporting engines
#
# OMITTED AT TIER 0, deliberately: `report-to csp`. ADU-SEC-01 section 5.2 lists
# it, but it only works alongside a `Reporting-Endpoints` header naming a real
# collector, and section 13.1's reporting endpoint is not provisioned yet. A
# `report-to` pointing at a group that does not exist is silently dead. It is
# added in the same change that stands the collector up (section 13.2 rollout).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ORIGINS ARE DERIVED, NOT TYPED (T23 Phase 4).
#
# What stood here was two example hostnames -- api.adulis.example and
# media.adulis.example -- with a note saying they would be "replaced with the
# real ones when DNS is provisioned". That is a second place to configure an
# origin the build already knows, and A POLICY HARDCODED IN ONE FILE AND AN
# ORIGIN CONFIGURED IN ANOTHER WILL DRIFT, SILENTLY. D-164 proved it in the
# sibling harness: the e2e server replayed this production directive at a
# localhost bundle, every fetch was refused by connect-src before it left the
# browser, and the suite went green against something else serving the port.
# D-276 was the same drift reaching boot-smoke.
#
# So the two origins are now PLACEHOLDERS filled at build time by
# ops/scripts/build-headers.mjs, from the SAME variables the bundle is built
# with -- VITE_API_BASE_URL and R2_MEDIA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN. One value, one place,
# and a build whose policy cannot disagree with the code it ships beside.
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE IS STILL WRITTEN, NOT ASSEMBLED, which is what
# section 3.9 requires: the policy is reviewable in a diff. Two origin tokens
# are substituted; no directive is added, removed or reordered, and the build
# step REFUSES to write a file with an unresolved placeholder rather than
# shipping a policy with a literal placeholder in it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' ${MEDIA_ORIGIN} blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' ${API_ORIGIN} ${MEDIA_ORIGIN}; frame-src 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-site
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
